@page "/agent-setup"

<PageTitle>Agent setup</PageTitle>

<h1>Agent setup</h1>

<p>
    Let a coding agent push a Terraform plan JSON straight into the page you have open, without the
    clipboard and without the plan passing through the model context.
</p>

<h4>How it works</h4>
<ol>
    <li>The agent opens <a href="">this site</a> in a browser tab.</li>
    <li>The page shows an <strong>agent code</strong> (element <code>#agent-code</code>).</li>
    <li>The agent sends the plan file to the API with that code as a bearer token.</li>
    <li>The plan lives only as long as that page is connected. When the connection drops, it is released after a short grace period (about 2 minutes). Refreshing the page gives a new code.</li>
</ol>

<h4>API</h4>
<pre class="bg-light p-3 border rounded"><code>POST /api/plan
Authorization: Bearer &lt;agent code&gt;
Content-Type: application/json

&lt;plan JSON, max 20 MB&gt;</code></pre>
<ul>
    <li><code>200</code> &mdash; loaded; body <code>{"resourceChanges": n}</code></li>
    <li><code>400</code> &mdash; not valid JSON; body <code>{"error": "..."}</code></li>
    <li><code>401</code> &mdash; unknown or expired code (read the current code from the open page)</li>
    <li><code>413</code> &mdash; plan too large</li>
    <li><code>429</code> &mdash; too many failed attempts from this address</li>
</ul>

<h4>Examples</h4>
<p>PowerShell:</p>
<pre class="bg-light p-3 border rounded"><code>Invoke-RestMethod -Method Post -Uri @(ApiUrl) `
    -Headers @@{ Authorization = "Bearer $code" } `
    -ContentType application/json -InFile terraform.plan.json</code></pre>
<p>curl:</p>
<pre class="bg-light p-3 border rounded"><code>curl -sS -X POST @(ApiUrl) \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer $CODE" \
    -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
    --data-binary @@terraform.plan.json</code></pre>

<h4>Looking ahead: WebMCP</h4>
<p>
    <a href="https://github.com/webmachinelearning/webmcp">WebMCP</a> is an emerging proposal that lets a web page
    expose tools to the browser's agent through <code>navigator.modelContext</code>. This app already registers a
    <code>load-terraform-plan</code> tool for it when the browser supports it, and the tool loads the plan through the
    same code path as the API.
</p>
<p>
    Once agent browsers expose WebMCP, an agent that has this page open can call that tool directly. There would be
    no code to read and no HTTP call to make. Until then, most agent browser panes do not support it, so the API above
    remains the way to go. It will stay available afterwards as a fallback for agents without WebMCP support.
</p>

<h4>One-time skill onboarding</h4>
<p>
    Copy the <code>semantic-compare-plan</code> skill from the
    <a href="https://github.com/cveld/terraform-blazor-semanticcompare/tree/main/.claude/skills/semantic-compare-plan">repository</a>
    to <code>~/.claude/skills/semantic-compare-plan</code> (or your agent's equivalent skills folder).
    After that, ask the agent to open a plan in Semantic Compare; it reads the code from the page and posts the file.
</p>

@code {
    [Inject] private NavigationManager Navigation { get; set; } = default!;

    private string ApiUrl => Navigation.ToAbsoluteUri("api/plan").ToString();
}
